# Cloudflare Pages redirects. Config, not content — Pages consumes this file
# and never serves it.
#
# A branded /support means the App Store listing can point at this domain
# rather than at whatever the support channel happens to be today; changing
# channels then becomes a commit here instead of an App Store metadata upload.
# /download is that idea applied to the disk image: a URL anyone may link
# that always hands back the current release, so an external link never has
# to be revisited.
#
# Both spellings are listed because a rule matches the exact path: /support
# would not catch /support/.
#
# GitHub Pages ignores this file, so these resolve on vibeplayer.app only.
# That is the canonical host, and the one the store links to.

/support   https://github.com/cmicali/vibe/issues   301
/support/  https://github.com/cmicali/vibe/issues   301

# The target names a version, so scripts/web-set-version.sh rewrites these
# three lines at release time from the same URL it writes into the page's
# Download button — one source, so the branded link and the button can never
# name different builds. GitHub's own /releases/latest/download shortcut
# cannot stand in: it only redirects for an asset name that never changes,
# and the default asset is named Vibe-macOS-universal-<version>.dmg. The arm64
# DMG remains an alternate on GitHub.
#
# TRAP: 302, never 301. The target moves every release, and a browser that
# cached a permanent redirect would keep fetching that one version forever,
# with nothing on this end able to correct it.

/download         https://github.com/cmicali/vibe/releases/download/v1.10/Vibe-macOS-1.10.dmg   302
/download/        https://github.com/cmicali/vibe/releases/download/v1.10/Vibe-macOS-1.10.dmg   302
/download/latest  https://github.com/cmicali/vibe/releases/download/v1.10/Vibe-macOS-1.10.dmg   302
